{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a chance-based wheel reward mechanic in the genie game. After the wheel has apparently stopped on a valuable prize, the game redirects it into another spin, and the resulting prize is lower. This explicitly alleges that the game manipulates the outcome rather than accepting the apparent result, which fits Illusion of Control. The wheel also delivers rewards through a variable, chance-based spin system without any stated monetary stake, so Reward Mania applies; Gambling does not, because the review does not describe real money or purchased currency being wagered.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the game changes the wheel outcome after it appears to have landed on a better prize, describing manipulated outcome determination."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners; variable or chance-based reward delivery",
      "rationale": "The game delivers prizes through a wheel-spin mechanism whose outcome determines the reward, without an explicit monetary stake."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review primarily attributes the inability to open gifts, trade, catch Pokémon, and conduct battles to technical faults: crashing, lag, unresponsive controls, and freezing. These malfunction descriptions do not trigger dark-pattern labels under R11. It also explicitly describes PvP battles in which an opponent attacks, which is an in-game competitive mechanic independent of the reported lag, so Competition applies. Gifts and trading with friends are mentioned, but no obligation to reciprocate is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponent keep attacking",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that make players against each other in competition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes a competitive battle involving the reviewer and an opponent."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-second connection delay before entering a match, but this is a matchmaking or service wait rather than an in-game timer, cooldown, or other designed wait-to-play mechanic. The reviewer also says rewards are merely for show and that weapon upgrades or perks are difficult to obtain, but does not describe a specific reward-delivery system, payment gate, repetitive activity, or concrete shortfall. The phrase \"battling to win\" explicitly describes player-versus-player competition, although the review does not describe harmful or deceptive competitive conduct. The complaint about losing time is an evaluation, not a separate mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "you stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes battling other players to achieve victory, which is a competitive game mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive play against other players, including repeated encounters with specific cards and defending against them. It also explicitly describes a pay-to-win structure in which skill alone is insufficient to climb higher, implying that spending provides an advantage for progression or winning. The complaints that certain cards are annoying, overused, or buffed by updates are balance evaluations, not evidence that purchased items lost value over time, so Power Creep does not apply. The need to use annoying tactics is ordinary competitive gameplay here and does not establish incentivized harmful or dishonest behavior.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes playing against other players using particular cards in trophy-based competition."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress; the indicator covers spending money to purchase an advantage, and the review links that advantage to climbing higher.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that payment provides a winning or progression advantage and that skill alone does not allow higher advancement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a pass as an in-game purchase, but does not explain its duration, renewal, or any pressure to play enough to justify it, so the pass reference alone does not establish Recurring Fee. The statement that a game element failed to appear within the move limits is attributed to a game-element failure and customer-service compensation issue, not a deliberate dark-pattern mechanic. The reviewer explicitly says payment is not required to win, which does not support Pay to Progress. The review does explicitly describe exchanging lives with existing friends or teammates, which is a resource-transfer mechanism creating reciprocity.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Reciprocity indicator: feeling obligated to return a favor or share resources; boundary rule distinguishes exchanging resources with people already playing from recruiting new players.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes trading lives with friends and teammates, which is an in-game resource exchange."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current mechanics. First, lives are replenished over time, and the reviewer wants the wait for a new life reduced to 5–10 minutes; this implies an in-game timer that limits when play can resume. Second, the game provides daily prizes and resets the player's progress in that reward sequence when they miss a day. The references to harder levels and celebrating tasks are not specific enough to establish grinding or another label. No web search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait for something, including lives or energy refilling over time.",
      "rationale": "The request to shorten the time for a new life implies that life-based play is currently gated by a regeneration timer."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars and the boundary rule requiring an active consequence for missing a day, such as a broken streak or reset reward sequence.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily prizes and resets the player's reward progress when they fail to return on a given day."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that the player cannot dismiss: there is no close control, tapping the ad redirects to the store, and returning to the game leaves the ad screen stuck until the app is terminated. This explicitly describes compelled ad exposure, so Advertisement applies. The absent close control and the fact that any tap routes to the store obstruct opting out and direct attention toward the purchase destination, so UI Misdirection also applies. The review does not say that terminating the game forfeits progress, so Mandatory Marathon does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "darkpattern.games Advertisement indicator: forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to remain in an in-game advertisement because it cannot be closed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: an opt-out, close, cancel, or exit control is absent, and the interface routes interaction toward the purchase option",
      "rationale": "The missing close control obstructs opting out, while tapping the ad directs the player to the store."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes accumulated in-game purchases and progress that would be lost if the game were deleted. This makes leaving or abandoning the game costly because the player would forfeit value already invested, and the reviewer explicitly says they do not want that loss to happen. The request to add saving is hypothetical and is not coded separately; the currently described loss of investment is the applicable mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: accumulated time, money, and progress make it difficult to abandon the game; the boundary requires the investment to operate on the player's decision to leave, which is shown by the reviewer not wanting to incur the loss.",
      "rationale": "Deleting the game would forfeit purchased and earned value, making disengagement costly and discouraging the player from leaving."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes strategy gameplay involving building and expanding an empire, plus real-time battles against other players. The explicit multiplayer combat mechanic satisfies Competition. The positive statements about immersion, excitement, graphics, storyline, and being hooked do not identify any additional coded dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "indicator: The game makes you compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes real-time battles between the player and other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progression becoming contingent on purchasing something: they say that to have any chance of progressing, a purchase is needed. The mention of having no extra energy identifies the resource context, but it does not explicitly describe an energy regeneration timer, cooldown, or waiting schedule, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. The review does not identify real-money currency conversion, a recurring fee, or a specific purchase interface.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span states that continued progress requires making a purchase, which is a payment gate on progression."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits play: after the player exhausts energy, they cannot progress until the energy has regenerated, and the full recovery timer is long. This is an in-game regeneration wait that controls when progress can resume, matching Playing by Appointment. The comments about there being little to do, wanting console play, a better storyline, bigger maps, and more customization are evaluations or requests rather than additional coded mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait, including lives or energy refilling over time; the boundary rule requires the span to describe the wait or regeneration schedule.",
      "rationale": "Running out of energy blocks further progress until a long recovery timer restores it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Poké Balls being consumed more quickly because Pokémon repeatedly break out during capture attempts, leaving the player needing to buy more to continue catching Pokémon. This supports Pay to Progress because payment is presented as necessary to overcome depletion of a gameplay resource and continue the core activity. The reviewer also alleges that capture outcomes worsen after purchases, implying that the game manipulates or tunes outcome determination rather than merely producing an unlucky streak, which supports Illusion of Control. The later claim that the reviewer did not write the review describes possible account misuse, not an in-game messaging or posting mechanic, so Friend Spam / Impersonation is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition and boundary rule: payment is described as necessary to overcome a resource barrier and continue gameplay.",
      "rationale": "The rapid depletion of Poké Balls makes purchasing more necessary to continue making captures, a core progression activity."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary: the span asserts that the game changes or manipulates outcome determination after purchases, rather than merely reporting an unlucky run.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer implies that capture success is deliberately worsened after purchasing, alleging hidden manipulation of the capture outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progression being blocked by difficult levels and tasks. The phrase \"unless you buy boosters\" explicitly presents spending as the route around otherwise impossible tasks, which makes payment necessary or strongly required for progress. The statement that passing hard levels takes a week describes slow progress, but it does not identify a repetitive activity, so it does not support Grinding. No other listed mechanic is clearly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The review contrasts impossible free progression with buying boosters, indicating that payment is needed to advance past game obstacles."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event with a reward they were close to earning: they had three steps remaining, ran out of dice, and saw that two days remained. After sleeping and logging back in, the event had changed, so the event schedule removed the remaining opportunity before the displayed window had elapsed. This is a game-controlled schedule affecting access to event progress and rewards. The review does not describe a daily login reward, a paid requirement, repetitive activity, or pressure to continue playing beyond the account of the lost event opportunity.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Playing by Appointment; where a reward expires or becomes unavailable on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or resulting loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event and its reward are controlled by a game-defined time window, and the event changed after the player returned despite the displayed two days remaining."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a social recruitment incentive: inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook is supposed to produce rewards, so the game uses friends as a route to in-game benefit. It also describes a chance-based betting mechanic: the player accumulated more than 500 dice, used the maximum bet, and received no favourable outcomes such as shutdowns or bank heists. The repeated duplicate stickers likewise describe unwanted repeated results from a variable reward system. The internet connection error is explicitly attributed to a technical fault, so it does not create a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator",
      "rationale": "The span describes inviting new people into the game in exchange for in-game rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK!",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling boundary: a chance-based mechanic with an explicit wager using an in-game stake",
      "rationale": "The player uses dice in a maximum-bet attempt and describes the outcome as luck-dependent, establishing a wager involving chance."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "No Shut downs and no bank heists. Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; repeated unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool",
      "rationale": "The span describes unpredictable outcomes and repeated duplicate sticker rewards, showing engagement with variable reward delivery rather than a fixed reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an interface behavior in which opening the game or entering the 1v1 area automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet table available from the player's coin balance. This directs the player's attention and default selection toward the costly option, while the reviewer says mis-clicks can cause unintended coin losses. The request for a Default Table Lock and for the lowest-bet table to open by default is hypothetical and is not coded. The claim that players are forced to buy real currency is not sufficiently specific to establish a separate currency-conversion or pay-to-progress mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect; boundary rule for attention-directing interface behavior",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically focuses and defaults to the highest-cost betting option, directing the player's selection away from safer lower-bet choices and facilitating accidental spending of coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a claimed hidden manipulation of random outcomes: the RNG is said to be obviously biased against the player. Under the Illusion of Control boundary, this is an assertion that the game determines or tunes outcomes rather than leaving them genuinely random. The review also describes poor reward economics and a level costing 2200 while returning barely that amount, but it does not explicitly state that payment is required to continue or progress, nor does it describe stranded currency or a specific purchase-to-currency conversion. Therefore those monetary labels are not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes, including a fixed or tuned outcome pattern.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game's random-number outcomes are deliberately biased against the player, implying manipulated rather than genuine randomness."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a game characterized as repetitive, but it does not name a repeated activity required for progress, so the general complaint that it is a grinder does not satisfy T_Grinding. The reviewer states that winning an event is effectively determined by spending money, including a reported total of 30k spent to win. This describes a monetary advantage purchased for competitive success, satisfying Pay to Win under M_PayToProgress. The review does not explicitly state that players compete against other players, nor that payment is required merely to continue progressing through the game, so S_Competition and a separate pay wall label are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money; a player can spend real money to purchase something that gives them an advantage in the game.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly attributes winning to payment rather than skill, describing money as the source of an in-game advantage."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several mechanics. Spending money provides gameplay advantages through a pay-to-win structure. Base upgrades use increasingly long in-game timers, imposing waits on progress. The game's progression has no meaningful endpoint because scaling continues indefinitely, which supports Infinite Treadmill. The UI is described as intentionally confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, supporting UI Misdirection because the interface is said to serve a monetization purpose against the player's interests. Finally, players are placed in conflict with other alliances through attacks and bullying, supporting Competition. The alliance pressure does not support Forced Fellowship because the review describes joining an alliance for protection from other players, not an obligation to avoid letting friends or teammates down.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes a structure in which paying players gain gameplay advantages."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times through in-game timers.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that base upgrades are governed by increasingly long in-game timers that delay progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: game progression continually expands without an attainable end state.",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling and the absence of a meaningful endpoint describe progression that continually recedes rather than being completed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule for UI Misdirection: assign when the review attributes interface design to a purpose that works against the player's intent, including making the interface confusing so the player buys.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly attributes the confusing UI to a deliberate attempt to push purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that make players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span describes players being placed in conflict with other alliances through attacks and bullying."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a daily-reward system that now requires completing a betting threshold: they cannot complete the daily rewards unless they bet 50,000. This directly matches Daily Rewards because the game provides a daily reward objective and withholds completion of that reward unless the player performs the required daily activity. The required activity is a bet, but the review does not explicitly establish that the 50,000 is purchased with real money; it is therefore not enough for Gambling under the stated monetary-stake rule. The timed rewards and their maximum amount are mentioned as resource values, not as a distinct dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and indicator: daily task or reward completion is conditional on performing an in-game activity.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily rewards but requires the player to complete a betting requirement to obtain or complete them."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a sticker-collection goal in which the final one or two stickers are effectively unavailable through ordinary play and require spending a large amount of money. That makes collection completion payment-dependent, so it supports Pay to Progress. The sticker album also supports Complete the Collection because the reviewer is pursuing the final missing items. The wording that the last stickers will never be obtained unless the player pays describes deliberate scarcity tied to purchase, supporting Deceptive Luxury. The statements about spending hundreds of dollars and completing a goal only rarely do not by themselves establish a chance-based gambling mechanic, a hidden rigging mechanism, or a reward-delivery system. The review-gating complaint concerns writing an app-store review and does not match any listed in-game label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "definition; payment is required to continue progress toward completing the sticker collection",
      "rationale": "The final collection items are described as unattainable through ordinary play unless the player pays substantially."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes pursuing the last missing stickers needed to complete an in-game collection."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Rarity Level / Artificial Scarcity; scarcity directly affects purchase and price",
      "rationale": "The final stickers are presented as artificially scarce and obtainable only through a costly purchase."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes difficult and slow level progression, with the player taking an average of 5–7 days per level and repeatedly running out of lives. However, it does not describe a specific repetitive task required for progress, so this is not Grinding. The key monetization mechanic is that buying add-ons is presented as necessary to get through the levels; the non-paying route is described as excessively time-consuming. The reviewer also mentions having wasted money and time, but does not describe those investments making it difficult to leave—instead, they are uninstalling—so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose does not apply. Running out of lives is not enough for Playing by Appointment because no regeneration wait or cooldown schedule is stated.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through a sane mind. it's pure time waste if you're not paying.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The review states that payment is necessary to get through the game and that the free route makes progress excessively time-consuming."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes misleading third-party advertising, but advertising outside the game is out of scope and does not receive T_Advertisement. Within the game, the reviewer must perform resource-gathering and construction activities before reaching the occasional puzzle content; these activities are characterized as chores and are named explicitly. This supports Grinding because repetitive gameplay tasks are required to access desired progress or content. No payment, waiting timer, social mechanic, or in-game advertisement is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "making an army from scratch, training, building, gathering resources then you may like this game. If you expected puzzles such as those in the ads for this game, you'll be doing chores in order to get to them",
      "rule_applied": "vs Gameplay described as repetitive; definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies army-building, training, construction, and resource gathering as chore-like tasks that must be performed to reach the puzzles."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review primarily complains that external advertisements show many pin-pull levels and mini-games that are rarely encountered in the actual game; under R13, misleading third-party advertising is out of scope and does not receive T_Advertisement. The reviewer also describes spending real money to obtain extra coins, which is a real-money-to-in-game-currency conversion and supports M_IntermediateCurrency. Being over level 4000 does not itself establish Grinding or Infinite Treadmill, and connection problems after updates are technical faults rather than Fragmented Downloads or another dark pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "I have also paid a lot for extra coins and things",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / direction-of-transaction boundary",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes paying real money to acquire extra in-game coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current in-game mechanics. First, purchase offers appear every time the player logs in and block access to gameplay until they are dealt with, which is an active buying prompt that intercepts play. Second, progressing or keeping up with the shifting competitive meta requires buying the newest bot with money rather than earning it, making payment a condition for advancement or competitive progress. The statements that the game is pay-to-win and that it is only enjoyable when new are evaluations or summaries, not separate mechanics. The mention of ads does not clearly state that the player is forced or rewarded to watch them, so it does not support Advertisement. The shifting meta also does not explicitly say that an already purchased bot loses value, so Power Creep is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rule_applied": "Prompted to Buy / boundary rule vs Purchase solicitation",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers appear at login and directly intercept access to gameplay before the player can play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
